WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:14.069 --> 00:00:16.652
(reggae music)

00:00:23.867 --> 00:00:25.324
- When people see Lee Perry,

00:00:25.324 --> 00:00:28.812
they're feeling this guy's
just soul pouring out.

00:00:28.812 --> 00:00:32.229
He hits on something that's just magical.

00:00:35.808 --> 00:00:37.521
- Lee Scratch Perry's a magician,

00:00:37.521 --> 00:00:40.422
he's a madman, he's a music
maker, he's one of a kind,

00:00:40.422 --> 00:00:42.786
there's never gonna be one like him again.

00:00:42.786 --> 00:00:44.441
When I work with Lee Scratch Perry

00:00:44.441 --> 00:00:45.941
as his bandleader,

00:00:47.078 --> 00:00:48.205
he was like an idol of mine.

00:00:48.205 --> 00:00:50.504
I never thought I would meet the guy.

00:00:50.504 --> 00:00:52.958
I didn't even know if he was alive.

00:00:52.958 --> 00:00:56.488
Scratch is kind of like Yoda,
but he's also like a child

00:00:56.488 --> 00:00:58.948
inside an 80-year-old man's body.

00:00:58.948 --> 00:00:59.781
- Yeaaah.

00:01:04.213 --> 00:01:06.380
Woo ooh, woo ooh, woo ooh.

00:01:08.586 --> 00:01:11.647
- [EMCH] In many ways, I feel
like he's underappreciated.

00:01:11.647 --> 00:01:14.592
He's a guy who has touched so many people,

00:01:14.592 --> 00:01:18.293
- Bob Marley, Paul McCartney,
The Clash, The Beastie Boys.

00:01:18.293 --> 00:01:20.859
Keith Richards once said
that Lee Scratch Perry

00:01:20.859 --> 00:01:23.442
was the Salvador Dali of music.

00:01:36.794 --> 00:01:39.877
- Boom, boom, boom, boom, boom, boom.

00:01:44.742 --> 00:01:47.417
- [EMCH] It's amazing to see
this guy who's 80-years-old,

00:01:47.417 --> 00:01:50.557
who's just like this
creative force of nature.

00:01:50.557 --> 00:01:53.277
He's never without a computer.

00:01:53.277 --> 00:01:56.776
Now, he's even said sometimes
the computer is part of him.

00:01:56.776 --> 00:01:58.866
He's constantly reacting
to what's around him

00:01:58.866 --> 00:02:01.768
and processing it and it comes back out

00:02:01.768 --> 00:02:03.918
in one way or another.

00:02:03.918 --> 00:02:06.001
(laughs)

00:02:07.135 --> 00:02:08.143
- He always talks about people

00:02:08.143 --> 00:02:11.226
being either scientists or magicians.

00:02:12.395 --> 00:02:13.926
Everything I'm doing with the computers

00:02:13.926 --> 00:02:18.097
and twisting the knobs, he's
like, "you're a scientist."

00:02:18.097 --> 00:02:21.597
(electronic reggae music)

00:02:23.394 --> 00:02:28.168
This is actually how I wrote
the bass line to this track.

00:02:28.168 --> 00:02:30.611
Around me is all this
gear from decades past,

00:02:30.611 --> 00:02:33.651
not unlike what Scratch
was using back in Jamaica.

00:02:33.651 --> 00:02:35.859
In the same way Scratch
used the mixing board

00:02:35.859 --> 00:02:39.390
as an instrument to shape
a new form of music,

00:02:39.390 --> 00:02:40.873
now we're using the computer to shape

00:02:40.873 --> 00:02:43.817
a new form of musical
performance, so it allows

00:02:43.817 --> 00:02:47.900
us to take all this on
tour and take it on stage.

00:02:49.363 --> 00:02:50.196
- [Voiceover] We're about to give you

00:02:50.196 --> 00:02:53.909
the vibes of Mr. Lee Scratch Perry.

00:02:53.909 --> 00:02:55.501
How does everyone feel about that?

00:02:55.501 --> 00:02:57.197
Does that sound all right?

00:02:57.197 --> 00:02:59.447
(applause)

00:03:01.987 --> 00:03:04.025
(swelling music)

00:03:04.025 --> 00:03:04.858
You guys ready?

00:03:04.858 --> 00:03:07.858
(slow reggae music)

00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:18.479
- [EMCH] Often times,
people see me on stage

00:03:18.479 --> 00:03:19.754
and they think I'm just DJing,

00:03:19.754 --> 00:03:21.126
but in fact, what I'm doing is

00:03:21.126 --> 00:03:23.793
very demanding on the computer.

00:03:23.793 --> 00:03:26.811
We're creating kind of a new
blueprint for live performance,

00:03:26.811 --> 00:03:30.107
because we're using a
combination of live instruments,

00:03:30.107 --> 00:03:32.060
elements of the music in the computer,

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:36.162
and of course, elements
of improvisation live.

00:03:36.162 --> 00:03:39.159
Every night we perform it, it's different.

00:03:39.159 --> 00:03:40.492
It's living art.

00:03:41.826 --> 00:03:44.237
The science, the nature,
and the spirituality

00:03:44.237 --> 00:03:46.487
all coming together in one.

00:03:47.682 --> 00:03:50.348
That's what anyone can learn
from Lee Scratch Perry,

00:03:50.348 --> 00:03:52.349
whether they like his music or not,

00:03:52.349 --> 00:03:54.878
is to look at the world
like a child and never stop

00:03:54.878 --> 00:03:59.045
experimenting and pushing
your creativity forward.

